---
// Android-only anatomy figure for "Overriding env(safe-area-inset-*) in a WebView".
//
// The base is a real screenshot: OnePlus 6T, Android 15, WebView 153, density 314,
// the launcher in edge-to-edge mode showing the post's debug page, three-button
// navigation, keyboard open, page theme forced to light. The regions are annotated
// in HTML over the image, so the badges scale with it and nothing is baked into
// the pixels.
//
// Hovering or focusing a numbered region lights up that region and its legend row
// together, which is the whole reason this is a component and not a picture: the
// mapping from name to part of the screen is the thing the figure is teaching.
//
// Every name here — window, system bars, navigationBars(), ime(), decor view,
// android.R.id.content — is Android's. iOS has a single safe area and none of this
// vocabulary, so the badge and the title are load-bearing: this figure must not be
// read as a cross-platform diagram.
//
// The region percentages are measured off the screenshot itself (1080x2340):
//   status bar  0.00% ..  3.42%   (80px  = the 41 CSS px top inset)
//   ime        70.68% .. 95.73%   (the keyboard, 1654px to the navigation bar)
//   nav bar    95.73% .. 100.0%   (the three-button bar, 100px)
// Re-measure them if the screenshot is ever replaced.
---
<figure class="aln">
  <figcaption class="aln-cap">
    <div class="aln-title">
      <span class="aln-badge">Android</span>
      <span>The parts of the screen, and what Android calls them</span>
    </div>
    <p class="aln-hint">Hover or focus a number to highlight it on the screen.</p>
  </figcaption>

  <div class="aln-body">
    <div class="aln-screen">
      <img
        class="aln-shot"
        src="/blog/posts/webview-safe-area-inset-override/android-screen-anatomy.webp"
        width="1080"
        height="2340"
        alt="A real Android screen: the status bar across the top, the page in the middle, the keyboard above the navigation bar at the bottom"
      />
      <div class="aln-win" data-n="1" aria-hidden="true">
        <button type="button" class="aln-n" aria-label="1: the app's window" aria-describedby="aln-row-1">1</button>
      </div>
      <div class="aln-region sb" data-n="2" aria-hidden="true">
        <button type="button" class="aln-n" aria-label="2: the system bars" aria-describedby="aln-row-2">2</button>
      </div>
      <div class="aln-region ime" data-n="4" aria-hidden="true">
        <button type="button" class="aln-n warn" aria-label="4: ime(), the keyboard inset" aria-describedby="aln-row-4">4</button>
      </div>
      <div class="aln-region nav" data-n="3" aria-hidden="true">
        <button type="button" class="aln-n warn" aria-label="3: navigationBars()" aria-describedby="aln-row-3">3</button>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="aln-stack">
      <div class="aln-hdr">On the screen</div>
      <div class="aln-box" id="aln-row-1" data-n="1">
        <span class="aln-n">1</span>
        <div><div class="aln-t">The app's window</div><div class="aln-d">The rectangle the screen draws into. This app is <i>edge-to-edge</i>, so that is the whole screen and the content sits behind the bars.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="aln-box" id="aln-row-2" data-n="2">
        <span class="aln-n">2</span>
        <div><div class="aln-t">The system bars</div><div class="aln-d">The status bar, the navigation bar and the caption bar. Android reports their sizes together as <code>systemBars()</code>.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="aln-box" id="aln-row-3" data-n="3">
        <span class="aln-n warn">3</span>
        <div><div class="aln-t">navigationBars()</div><div class="aln-d">The bottom bar on its own. With the keyboard up it reports the keyboard's height, which is where the double-count in this post starts.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="aln-box" id="aln-row-4" data-n="4">
        <span class="aln-n warn">4</span>
        <div><div class="aln-t">ime()</div><div class="aln-d">The keyboard's own inset. The WebView reads it separately from the system bars and uses it to resize the visual viewport.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="aln-hdr">Inside the window, outermost first</div>
      <div class="aln-box">
        <span class="aln-n dim">5</span>
        <div><div class="aln-t">The decor view</div><div class="aln-d">Android's frame inside the window: the title bar, if the app has one, plus the content frame.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="aln-box">
        <span class="aln-n dim">6</span>
        <div><div class="aln-t">android.R.id.content</div><div class="aln-d">The frame Android puts <i>your</i> layout into. Gadgetbridge adds padding to <b>this</b> view, and that padding is what causes the problem.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="aln-box">
        <span class="aln-n dim">7</span>
        <div><div class="aln-t">The app's layout</div><div class="aln-d">Whatever the app puts inside that frame. In this launcher it is a <code>ConstraintLayout</code> holding nothing but the WebView; Gadgetbridge wraps a <code>LinearLayout</code> around it.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="aln-box">
        <span class="aln-n dim">8</span>
        <div><div class="aln-t">The WebView</div><div class="aln-d">The browser widget the page runs in. It has its own opinion about the keyboard, which is the second half of the problem.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</figure>

<style>
  .aln {
    /* Centred block, not full-bleed: the prose column is ~56rem wide and a
       full-width figure reads as a band rather than a figure.

       width:100% is load-bearing. The site's `.markdown > *` rule sets
       width:56rem and clamps it with max-width:calc(100% - 2rem); overriding
       that max-width without also resetting the width leaves the figure 704px
       wide on a phone, overflowing the column. */
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 44rem;
    margin: 2rem auto;
    padding: 1rem 1.15rem 1.25rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
    border-radius: 0.75rem;
    background: rgba(var(--gray-light), 0.28);
  }
  .aln-cap { margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .aln-title {
    display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.5rem; flex-wrap: wrap;
    font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.3;
    color: rgb(var(--gray-dark));
  }
  .aln-badge {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    font-size: 0.7rem; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.06em; text-transform: uppercase;
    padding: 0.15rem 0.45rem; border-radius: 999px;
    background: var(--accent); color: #fff;
  }
  .aln-hint { margin: 0.35rem 0 0; font-size: 0.8rem; color: rgb(var(--gray)); }

  .aln-body { display: flex; gap: 1.5rem; align-items: flex-start; }

  /* The screenshot. Everything else in here is positioned in percentages of the
     image box, so the annotations track it at any width. */
  .aln-screen {
    position: relative;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 300px;
    aspect-ratio: 1080 / 2340;
    border-radius: 0.6rem;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: #0b0b0d;
    /* lets the badges be sized in cqw so they scale with the image */
    container-type: inline-size;
  }
  .aln-shot { display: block; width: 100%; height: 100%; object-fit: cover; }

  /* The window is the whole screen, so its outline hugs the image. It takes no
     pointer events of its own: hovering the page area should not fire it, only
     its badge should. */
  .aln-win {
    position: absolute; inset: 2px;
    border: 2px dashed var(--aln-line);
    border-radius: 0.5rem;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* The two system bars and the keyboard, outlined where they actually are. These
     use a border rather than an inset box-shadow so the window, the regions and
     the legend rows all fade through the one border-color property. */
  .aln-region { position: absolute; left: 0; right: 0; border: 2px solid var(--aln-line); }
  .aln-region.sb  { top: 0;       height: 3.42%; }
  .aln-region.ime { top: 70.68%;  height: 25.05%; }
  .aln-region.nav { top: 95.73%;  height: 4.27%; }

  /* Badges. In the legend they are plain circles; on the screenshot they are
     buttons, so a keyboard user can tab through them and see each region light up. */
  .aln-n {
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    width: 1.05rem; height: 1.05rem; flex: 0 0 1.05rem;
    border-radius: 50%; background: var(--accent); color: #fff;
    font-size: 0.68rem; font-weight: 700;
  }
  .aln-n.warn { background: var(--tx-point); color: #fff; }
  .aln-n.dim { background: rgb(var(--gray-light)); color: rgb(var(--gray-dark)); }
  .aln-screen .aln-n {
    position: absolute; pointer-events: auto; cursor: pointer;
    appearance: none; border: 0; padding: 0; line-height: 1;
    /* rem first, then the proportional size where container queries exist */
    width: 1.05rem; height: 1.05rem;
    width: 5.4cqw; height: 5.4cqw;
    font-size: 0.68rem;
    font-size: 3.4cqw;
  }
  .aln-screen .aln-n:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid rgb(var(--white)); outline-offset: 2px; }
  .aln-win  .aln-n { left: 4%; top: 44%; }
  .aln-region.sb  .aln-n { left: 5%; top: 50%; transform: translateY(-50%); }
  .aln-region.ime .aln-n { left: 5%; top: 0.5rem; }
  .aln-region.nav .aln-n { left: 5%; top: 50%; transform: translateY(-50%); }

  .aln-stack { flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.45rem; }
  .aln-hdr {
    margin-top: 0.35rem;
    font-size: 0.68rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.09em;
    color: rgb(var(--gray));
  }
  .aln-hdr:first-child { margin-top: 0; }
  .aln-box {
    display: flex; gap: 0.65rem; align-items: flex-start;
    padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); border-radius: 0.6rem;
    background: rgb(var(--white));
  }
  .aln-t { font-weight: 600; font-size: 0.88rem; color: rgb(var(--gray-dark)); }
  .aln-d { margin-top: 0.1rem; font-size: 0.82rem; line-height: 1.4; color: rgb(var(--gray)); }
  .aln-d code {
    font-family: var(--font-family-mono);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  /* Hover / focus pairing. The pair is selected by the shared data-n, so pointing
     at either a region or its legend row lights up both, and the name and the part
     of the screen are always shown together.

     The hover and focus target is the .aln-box rectangle itself. Nothing inside it
     is a trigger and nothing inside it is styled separately: the whole row fades as
     one rectangle.

     The screenshot's regions cannot fade with opacity, because a child cannot be
     more opaque than its parent and the numbered buttons have to stay at full
     strength. They fade their border instead, which is why the regions and the
     window use a border rather than an inset shadow.

     The dimming rules sit in :where(), which gives them no specificity, so the
     highlight rules always win for the pair itself.

     Hover is behind (hover: hover) and (pointer: fine): on a touch device a tap
     leaves a stuck :hover, and the figure should not light up at random after one.
     Focus works everywhere, which is also what makes this reachable by keyboard. */
  [data-n="1"], [data-n="2"] { --aln-c: var(--accent); }
  [data-n="3"], [data-n="4"] { --aln-c: var(--tx-point); }

  .aln-region.sb { --aln-line: var(--accent); --aln-line-dim: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 30%, transparent); }
  .aln-region.ime, .aln-region.nav { --aln-line: var(--tx-point); --aln-line-dim: color-mix(in srgb, var(--tx-point) 30%, transparent); }
  .aln-win { --aln-line: var(--accent); --aln-line-dim: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 30%, transparent); }

  .aln-box[data-n] { border-color: var(--aln-c); }

  .aln :is(.aln-region, .aln-win, .aln-box) {
    transition: background-color .14s ease, border-color .14s ease, opacity .14s ease;
  }
  .aln :is(.aln-region, .aln-win, .aln-box)[data-n] { outline: 2px solid transparent; outline-offset: -2px; }

  /* Focus: every device. */
  .aln:has([data-n="1"]:focus-within) [data-n="1"],
  .aln:has([data-n="2"]:focus-within) [data-n="2"],
  .aln:has([data-n="3"]:focus-within) [data-n="3"],
  .aln:has([data-n="4"]:focus-within) [data-n="4"] {
    background-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--aln-c) 16%, transparent);
    border-color: var(--aln-line, var(--aln-c));
    outline-color: var(--aln-c);
    opacity: 1;
  }
  :where(.aln:has([data-n]:focus-within)) .aln-box:where(:not(:focus-within)) { opacity: .4; }
  :where(.aln:has([data-n]:focus-within))
    :where(.aln-region, .aln-win):where(:not(:focus-within)) { border-color: var(--aln-line-dim); }

  /* Hover: only where a pointer can actually hover. */
  @media (hover: hover) and (pointer: fine) {
    .aln:has([data-n="1"]:hover) [data-n="1"],
    .aln:has([data-n="2"]:hover) [data-n="2"],
    .aln:has([data-n="3"]:hover) [data-n="3"],
    .aln:has([data-n="4"]:hover) [data-n="4"] {
      background-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--aln-c) 16%, transparent);
      border-color: var(--aln-line, var(--aln-c));
      outline-color: var(--aln-c);
      opacity: 1;
    }
    :where(.aln:has([data-n]:hover)) .aln-box:where(:not(:hover)):where(:not(:focus-within)) { opacity: .4; }
    :where(.aln:has([data-n]:hover))
      :where(.aln-region, .aln-win):where(:not(:hover)):where(:not(:focus-within)) { border-color: var(--aln-line-dim); }
  }

  /* Narrow screens: stack, and centre the screenshot. It keeps its aspect ratio,
     so it simply scales down and the annotations scale with it. */
  @media (max-width: 40rem) {
    .aln { padding: 0.85rem 0.85rem 1rem; }
    .aln-body { flex-direction: column; gap: 1rem; }
    .aln-screen { width: 100%; max-width: 250px; margin-inline: auto; }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .aln :is(.aln-region, .aln-win, .aln-box) { transition: none; }
  }
</style>
